<% content_for :title, "Publishing — Omarchy Plugins" %>
<div class="content-page container">
  <p class="eyebrow">Publishing guide</p>
  <h1>Ship a plugin in minutes.</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="statement">Push it anywhere — GitHub, Codeberg, your own Forgejo, or nowhere
      public at all. The registry never requires a forge. Tag, publish, done.</p>

    <h2>1. Scaffold</h2>
    <pre>$ omarchy plugin new weather</pre>
    <p>You get a <code>manifest.json</code>, a starter widget, a readme, and an optional CI
      publish workflow.</p>

    <h2>2. Claim your namespace</h2>
    <p><%= link_to "Sign in", new_session_path %> (no password — we email you a code), claim
      your handle, and enable two-factor auth. Your plugin's id will be
      <code>handle.name</code>, installed as <code>handle/name</code>.</p>

    <h2>3. Publish</h2>
    <pre>$ omarchy plugin publish
# device flow: approve the 8-char code in your browser, done.
# or, by hand:
$ tar czf weather-1.0.0.tar.gz manifest.json Widget.qml README.md
$ curl -X POST --data-binary @weather-1.0.0.tar.gz \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer omp_…" \
    https://plugins.omarchy.org/api/v1/plugins/you/weather/versions</pre>
    <p>A publish token from <code>omarchy plugin publish</code> is push-only, covers any plugin in
      your namespaces, and expires in 7 days (CI tokens from trusted publishing are minted per
      plugin). Push-only means it can't yank, transfer ownership, or change account settings. Every version —
      first publish or update — runs the same review pipeline: structural validation, malware
      scanning, a capability fingerprint, and a short hold before going live. Versions are
      immutable: once <code>you/weather@1.0.0</code> exists, those bytes are frozen forever.</p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Strict semver, always increasing. A used version number is burned forever, even if rejected.</li>
      <li><code>license</code> (SPDX) is required. <code>repository</code> is optional and shown on your plugin page — publishing via trusted publishing adds provenance links to the exact commit.</li>
      <li>No symlinks, no entry points outside the plugin directory, 10 MB max.</li>
      <li>Manifest <code>id</code> must equal <code>yourhandle.pluginname</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Prefer hands-off?</h2>
    <p>Register <span class="term">trusted publishing</span> from your dashboard (repo +
      workflow + environment, GitHub Actions today) and tagging <code>v1.0.0</code> publishes
      for you via OIDC with no stored secret at all — your plugin page gets provenance links
      back to the exact commit and workflow run.</p>
  </div>
</div>
